        "student_prefix": [
          {
            "content": "Conner has a dune buggy that he rides in the desert.  On flat sand, it can ride at a speed of 60 miles per hour.  When traveling on downhill slopes, it can race at 12 miles per hour faster than it can when it is on flat sand.  And when riding on an uphill inclined slow, it travels at a speed 18 miles per hour slower than when it is riding on flat sand.  If Conner rides his dune buggy one-third of the time on flat sand, one-third of the time on uphill slopes, and one-third of the time on downhill slopes, what is his average speed in miles per hour?",
            "role": "user"
          },
          {
            "content": "Given:\n- Speed on flat sand: 60 mph\n- Speed on downhill slopes: 60 mph + 12 mph = 72 mph\n- Speed on uphill slopes: 60 mph - 18 mph = 42 mph\n- Time spent on each: 1/3 of total time\n\nTo find the average speed, we use the formula for average speed, which is the total distance divided by the total time. Since the time spent on each type of terrain is equal, we can simply average the speeds.\n\nAverage speed = (Speed on flat sand + Speed on downhill slopes + Speed on uphill slopes) / 3\nAverage speed = (60 mph + 72 mph + 42 mph) / 3\nAverage speed = 174 mph / 3\nAverage speed = 58 mph\n\nFINAL: 58",
            "role": "assistant"
          },
          {
            "content": "Write your own reusable record of this solved task.",
            "role": "user"
          }
        ],
        "student_prefix_sha256": "1b92e80f775c0dfc2bfd1a768dcfce904df95fa2cb62e4345ba26046631cb2b8",
        "target": "Given the problem, I identified the key quantities and their relationships:\n- Speed on flat sand: 60 mph\n- Speed on downhill slopes: 60 mph + 12 mph = 72 mph\n- Speed on uphill slopes: 60 mph - 18 mph = 42 mph\n- Time spent on each: 1/3 of total time\n\nTo find the average speed, I used the formula for average speed, which is the total distance divided by the total time. Since the time spent on each type of terrain is equal, I decided to average the speeds directly. This approach is valid because the time spent on each terrain is the same, so the average speed is simply the arithmetic mean of the speeds.\n\nI calculated the average speed as follows:\n\\[ \\text{Average speed} = \\frac{60 \\text{ mph} + 72 \\text{ mph} + 42 \\text{ mph}}{3} \\]\n\\[ \\text{Average speed} = \\frac{174 \\text{ mph}}{3} \\]\n\\[ \\text{Average speed} = 58 \\text{ mph} \\]\n\nTo check the result, I verified that the calculation was correct by ensuring that the sum of the speeds divided by 3 indeed equals 58 mph. I also confirmed that the individual speeds and the time spent on each type of terrain were correctly identified and used in the calculation.\n\nThe operation I would reuse is averaging the speeds when the time spent on each type of terrain is equal. This method is applicable whenever the time spent on different conditions is the same and the speeds vary.\n\nFINAL: 58",
        "target_sha256": "d9a8ab6e8238e3e443f52f2654109fc691902f425a547d7a26f6541106e7dce9"
